  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Five Card Stud. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Five Card Stud is one of the earliest forms of Stud Poker, emerging in the mid-19th century. Unlike Draw Poker, Five Card Stud is an 'open' game where most of a player's hand is visible to everyone at the table. Each player receives one hidden 'hole' card and four exposed 'up' cards, with betting rounds interspersed between each deal. This creates a high-tension environment where players must calculate their strength against the visible threats in their opponents' hands.",
    objective: 'Win the pot by having the best 5-card poker hand or by being the last player remaining after everyone else folds.',
    gameplay: '1. 1st Street: Each player gets one card face-down (hole) and one card face-up. Betting round begins with the lowest upcard (Bring-In).\n2. 2nd Street: A second upcard is dealt. Betting round begins with the highest visible hand.\n3. 3rd Street: A third upcard is dealt. Betting round begins with the highest visible hand.\n4. 4th Street: The final upcard is dealt. Final betting round.\n5. Showdown: hole cards are revealed.',
    keyRules: [
      'The Bring-In: Unlike blinds, the first bet is forced on the player with the worst visible start (the lowest card).',
      "Street nomenclature: The rounds are often called 'Third Street' (the first 2 cards) through 'Fifth Street' (the final card).",
    ],
